{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788201263,"nonce":"MTUqWjmMOWzrNp0G/qsUVxP37+w=","title":"map-view publishes a legend but no alphabet identity, so the ASCII and PNG channels cannot be keyed against each other","message":"Found by W1 (autorimmer-27) while vetting f7b6207, session 9. Routed out\nbecause Spatial.cs is outside that worker's territory.\n\n## The gap\n\nComment #4 on the 2.5 spec makes emitting an alphabet identity a standing\ninstruction, and the new `map-dump` verb will carry one. `map-view` does not:\nit publishes `legend` but no alphabet NAME.\n\nSo a consumer holding both channels — the ASCII view and the PNG render of the\nsame rect — cannot prove they were drawn with the same symbol table. The whole\npoint of the PNG channel is to be an INDEPENDENT SECOND CHECK on textual\nspatial reasoning (2.5's goal). An independent check whose agreement with the\nfirst channel cannot be verified is worth much less: when the two disagree,\nthere is no way to tell a real divergence from two different alphabets.\n\n## Fix\n\n`map-view` emits the same alphabet identity `map-dump` does, so the two are\ncomparable by name rather than by assumption. Lands in `Spatial.cs`.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `map-view` publishes an alphabet identity in the same field and format as\n  `map-dump`.\n- Two renders of the same rect through the two channels can be shown to share\n  an alphabet by comparing that field alone, with no out-of-band knowledge.\n- A change to the symbol table changes the identity, so a stale consumer can\n  detect it rather than silently misread.","files":null}]}